+### 18.2 The perturbation argument with these counts (N1, K6; 7.3, R6)
+
+- **Start phases (7.3 bullet 1).** Against the tick on which the thread wakes,
+  the 0x33's S5 now comes ≈ 553 instructions (≈ 2.5 µs) later (tick hook 48,
+  wake-up 109, switch-in 203, `psc_poll_begin` 95, loop top 7, inline hooks
+  ≈ 16, entry 75), not ≈ 0.3-0.6 µs; the 0x08's S5 ≈ 1,119 (≈ 5.1 µs) later,
+  not ≈ 2.4-3.4 µs; a command resumed after a Nop ≈ 5.0 µs later, not ≈ 2-4
+  µs. **(K6)** The change in the chance that a given Nop lands in a given
+  window is at most δ × (the largest density of window starts or ends per unit
+  of tick phase near the Nop's tick edge). That is δ/T (≤ 0.13 % with δ =
+  5.1 µs) only when the phases are spread; the thread wakes on a tick, so its
+  phases are concentrated, and the bound is the measured density, which the
+  decoder prints (18.5). Cache misses can make δ larger (UNVERIFIED).
+- **G3-low gap (7.3 bullet 4, R6).** +2.56 µs over a baseline gap of ≈ 252
+  instructions plus 10 MMIO accesses: the instruction part roughly triples
+  (G1-ruling red team EX1). Whether the 5 µs that the commented-out wait hints
+  at (`syscon.c:95-97`, `:142-144`) lies between the gap with and without the
+  instrumentation depends on the MMIO latency m (UNVERIFIED); the decoder now
+  computes an upper bound of m from the run's own shortest command and prints
+  both gaps (18.5, K5). It stays **the one direction in which the
+  instrumentation could hide a failure.**
+- **R6 (G4L).** A level-sensitive G4L pulse shorter than ≈ 5 µs (not ≈ 2-4 µs)
+  could be missed on the resumed thread; judged unlikely as before (separate
+  acknowledge write, `syscon.c:159`).
+- **Conclusion (7.3 bullet 5) unchanged:** nothing is added where a Nop can
+  land, every phase and gap is recorded per command, and the shift is small
+  against the wake-up delays that load already causes (`wk_delay`, `pre_tot`).
